# Property Price Prediction with Analytics & Recommendations

Bengaluru residential price modelling, an analytics module and a content-based
recommender — rebuilt from a joint course capstone into a reproducible package with an
audited train/evaluate path, a FastAPI scoring service, a Streamlit dashboard, Docker and CI.

> **Attribution.** The original analysis was joint work with a course partner; the source
> notebooks are preserved unchanged in `notebooks/`. This repository is the rebuild: the
> leakage guard, grouped split, dual-space metrics, recommender evaluation, serving layer
> and test suite are new.

---

## Why this repo exists

The capstone reported **R² = 0.82 for XGBoost**. That figure is not wrong so much as
underspecified, and the audit found three reasons it cannot be quoted as-is:

1. It is **R² on `log1p(price)`**, not on price. The target's skew is 6.53.
2. It came from a **shuffled split**, while 14.3% of rows duplicate another row's feature
   vector under a different price — so part of the test set was memorised.
3. It is the **maximum of 50 `RandomizedSearchCV` draws**, which is optimistic by
   construction, not a held-out score.

None of that means the model is bad. It means the number needed re-deriving before it
could go on a CV. The full write-up is in **[`reports/data_audit.md`](reports/data_audit.md)**.

## The findings that survived

**`price_per_sqft` is the target in disguise.** `price_per_sqft == price * 1e7 / area`
holds to a maximum deviation of **0.50** across all 10,626 rows — pure rounding. Handed
both columns, a model reconstructs the target at R² > 0.9999 and reports it as a result.
The original notebook drops them; here the drop is *enforced* — `Config.validate` refuses
to load a config listing them, `build_matrix` raises `LeakageError`, and CI runs the guard
as its own step so the build fails if anyone re-adds them.

**1,520 rows (14.3%) share a feature vector with another row.** No two rows are identical,
but 1,520 are indistinguishable on every modelling feature while carrying different prices.
`GroupShuffleSplit` over the duplicate-feature group keeps each cluster wholly in one fold.
The pipeline runs the leaderboard under *both* splits and records the gap, so the optimism
is a measured quantity rather than a caveat.

## Results

Run `make train`. It writes, and this README points at rather than restates:

| File | Contents |
|---|---|
| `reports/leaderboard.csv` | 9 regressors + 2 constant baselines, grouped split, both metric spaces |
| `reports/leaderboard_naive_split.csv` | The same leaderboard under the notebook's shuffled split |
| `reports/predictions.md` | The three pre-registered predictions and whether each held |
| `reports/summary.json` | Tuned model, held-out test scores, recommender scores |
| `reports/data_audit.json` | Machine-readable audit figures |

Three predictions are stated in `train.py` **before** the run — that the grouped split
scores worse, that `r2_price < r2_log`, and that XGBoost wins by a smaller margin than the
notebook's 0.809-vs-0.789 — and `predictions.md` reports mechanically whether each held.

## Recommender

The original recommender used cosine similarity and reported no metric, so "it recommends
similar properties" was untestable. There are no relevance labels here, so `recommend.py`
states a proxy up front — *a recommendation is relevant if it shares the query's location
and sits within 25% of its price* — and scores precision@k, location hit-rate and median
price gap against it. The proxy is arguable; it is written in the code so it can be argued
with.

## Quickstart

```bash
make setup          # venv + deps + editable install
make audit          # reproduce every figure in reports/data_audit.md
make train          # leaderboard, tuning, artefacts, reports
make test           # pytest + coverage
make api            # FastAPI on :8000  (/docs for OpenAPI)
make app            # Streamlit on :8501
docker compose up   # both services
```

On Windows there is no `make`; use the PowerShell wrapper instead — `.\tasks.ps1 setup`, `.\tasks.ps1 train`, `.\tasks.ps1 test`. See [`RUNME.md`](RUNME.md).

## API

| Method | Route | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| GET | `/health` | Liveness and whether artefacts are present |
| GET | `/metadata` | The model's measured metrics, served next to the model |
| POST | `/predict` | One property → price in crore |
| POST | `/predict/batch` | Up to 1,000 properties |
| GET | `/recommend/{index}` | Top-k similar listings |

Pydantic bounds reject impossible inputs (0 bedrooms, 10 sqft) with 422 rather than
returning a confident number for nonsense.

## Layout

```
config/config.yaml            single source of truth; validated on load
src/property_price/
  config.py                   typed config + banned-feature validation
  data.py                     grouped vs shuffled splits
  audit.py                    every number in reports/data_audit.md
  features.py                 preprocessor + the leakage guard
  models.py                   model zoo + search space
  evaluate.py                 metrics in log AND price space
  recommend.py                recommender + its evaluation protocol
  train.py                    orchestration; writes all reports
app/api/main.py               FastAPI service
app/streamlit_app.py          4-tab dashboard
tests/                        leakage guard, split integrity, metrics, recommender, API
notebooks/                    original capstone notebooks, unmodified
```

## Tests

`pytest` covers the leakage guard (including a guard-on-the-guard that fails if
`Config.validate` stops rejecting banned features), split integrity (no duplicate group
straddles the boundary), hand-computed metric fixtures, recommender sanity, and API
contract behaviour without a model artefact present.

## Data

`data/raw/final_data.csv` (10,626 × 11) is the cleaned frame from the original notebooks;
`Bengaluru_House_Data.csv` is the raw 13,320-row source. `latlong.csv` provides
coordinates for 242 locations used by the analytics map.

